import Bitcoin.Serialization.Interface

/-!
# Y01 property-test corpus — Serialization/CompactSize (TXV-012)

Review-4 support suite contributor Y01, 2026-10-16. NEW file, owned by Y01.

Ground truth: Bitcoin Core `serialize.h` @ `c50817f` (fetched from
github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin at that pinned commit):
`MAX_SIZE = 0x02000000`; `WriteCompactSize` (< 253 / ≤ 0xffff / ≤ 0xffffffff
framing); `ReadCompactSize` with `range_check = true`, non-canonical throw
BEFORE the range check. The corpus was generated by a plain-python
reference (`struct`-packing; scratch/Y01/cs_reference.py, seed 20261016,
REPRODUCIBLE) and is independent of the Lean implementation.

Structure (≈ 166 cases, all kernel `decide`-checkable concrete vectors):
  A  canonical `encCSL` vs python `WriteCompactSize` (systematic edges)
  B  canonical `decCS` vs python `ReadCompactSize` (with remainder)
  C  non-canonical / too-large rejects (TXV-012 reject side)
  D  deterministic `decCS ∘ encCSL` roundtrips
  E  truncated inputs
  F1 pseudo-random valid values (fixed seed 20261016)
  F2 pseudo-random wire strings, full-verdict equality (fixed seed)
  G  public `decodeCompactSize` ByteArray API vs Core divergence probes
  H  `encodeCompactSize`/`decodeCompactSize` API roundtrips
  I  UInt64-wrap edge of `encCSL` (documented typing artifact)

TWO Lean-vs-Core divergences were found and are pinned here with BOTH
values (see findings-w4/Y01.md):
  * F-Y01-005 (HIGH): `decCS` applies the MAX_SIZE range check ONLY to the
    0xff frame — Core `ReadCompactSize` range-checks EVERY frame, so
    0xfe-framed values in (MAX_SIZE, 0xffffffff] are `.ok` in Lean but
    "size too large" errors in Core. Divergent cases carry a
    `DIVERGENCE` comment and assert the Lean-actual value.
  * F-Y01-001 (LOW): public ByteArray `decodeCompactSize` (C1 bootstrap
    decoder) has NO canonicality / MAX_SIZE checks at all — section G.
All other 158 cases AGREE with the python ground truth (asserted
directly against the Core verdict).
-/


